"CSS Syntax Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/)\nciteturn2445view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/#consume-a-function\",\"lineno\":1314}); Total lines: 2053\nL1269: #### 5.5.8. Consume a component value\nL1270: \nL1271: To consume a component value from a 【237†token stream】 `input`:\nL1272: \nL1273: 【269†Process】 `input`:\nL1274: \nL1275: 【131†<{-token>】\nL1276: 【203†<[-token>】\nL1277: 【195†<(-token>】\nL1278: \nL1279: \nL1280: 【337†Consume a simple block】 from `input` and return the result.\nL1281: \nL1282: 【155†<function-token>】\nL1283: \nL1284: \nL1285: 【338†Consume a function】 from `input` and return the result.\nL1286: \nL1287: anything else\nL1288: \nL1289: \nL1290: 【308†Consume a token】 from `input` and return the result.\nL1291: #### 5.5.9. Consume a simple block\nL1292: \nL1293: To consume a simple block from a 【237†token stream】 `input`:\nL1294: \nL1295: Assert: the 【265†next token】 of `input` is 【131†<{-token>】, 【203†<[-token>】, or 【195†<(-token>】.\nL1296: \nL1297: Let `ending token` be the mirror variant of the 【265†next token】. (E.g. if it was called with 【203†<[-token>】, the `ending token` is 【208†<]-token>】.)\nL1298: Let `block` be a new 【249†simple block】 with its associated token set to the 【265†next token】 and with its value initially set to an empty 【259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL1299: \nL1300: 【268†Discard a token】 from `input`.\nL1301: \nL1302: 【269†Process】 `input`:\nL1303: \nL1304: 【153†<eof-token>】\nL1305: `ending token`\nL1306: \nL1307: \nL1308: 【268†Discard a token】 from `input`. Return `block`.\nL1309: \nL1310: anything else\nL1311: \nL1312: \nL1313: 【304†Consume a component value】 from `input` and append the result to `block`’s value.\nL1314: #### 5.5.10. Consume a function\nL1315: \nL1316: To consume a function from a 【237†token stream】 `input`:\nL1317: \nL1318: Assert: The 【265†next token】 is a 【155†<function-token>】.\nL1319: \nL1320: 【308†Consume a token】 from `input`, and let `function` be a new function with its name equal the returned token’s value, and a value set to an empty 【259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL1321: \nL1322: 【269†Process】 `input`:\nL1323: \nL1324: 【153†<eof-token>】\nL1325: 【196†<)-token>】\nL1326: \nL1327: \nL1328: 【268†Discard a token】 from `input`. Return `function`.\nL1329: \nL1330: anything else\nL1331: 【304†Consume a component value】 from `input` and append the result to `function`’s value.\nL1332: #### 5.5.11. Consume a 【236†unicode-range】 value\nL1333: \nL1334: To consume the value of a unicode-range descriptor from a string `input string`:\nL1335: \nL1336:   1. Let `tokens` be the result of 【270†tokenizing】 `input string` with `unicode ranges allowed` set to true.\nL1337: \nL1338:   2. 【305†Consume a list of component values】 from `tokens`, and return the result.\nL1339: \nL1340: Note: The existence of this algorithm is due to a design mistake in early CSS. It should never be reproduced.\nL1341: ## 6. The `An+B` microsyntax\nL1342: \nL1343: Several things in CSS, such as the 【339†:nth-child()】 pseudoclass, need to indicate indexes in a list. The `An+B` microsyntax is useful for this, allowing an author to easily indicate single elements or all elements at regularly-spaced intervals in a list.\nL1344: \nL1345: The An+B notation defines an integer step (`A`) and offset (`B`), and represents the `An+B`th elements in a list, for every positive integer or zero value of `n`, with the first element in the list having index 1 (not 0).\nL1346: For values of `A` and `B` greater than 0, this effectively divides the list into groups of `A` elements (the last group taking the remainder), and selecting the `B`th element of each group.\nL1347: \nL1348: The `An+B` notation also accepts the even and odd keywords, which have the same meaning as 2n and 2n+1, respectively.\nL1349: \nL1350: Examples:\nL1351: \nL1352:     2n+0   /* represents all of the even elements in the list */\nL1353:     even   /* same */\nL1354:     4n+1   /* represents the 1st, 5th, 9th, 13th, etc. elements in the list */\nL1355: The values of `A` and `B` can be negative, but only the positive results of `An+B`, for `n` ≥ 0, are used.\nL1356: \nL1357: Example:\nL1358: \nL1359:     -1n+6   /* represents the first 6 elements of the list */\nL1360:     -4n+10  /* represents the 2nd, 6th, and 10th elements of the list */\nL1361: \nL1362: \nL1363: If both `A` and `B` are 0, the pseudo-class represents no element in the list.\nL1364: ### 6.1. Informal Syntax Description\nL1365: \nL1366: This section is non-normative.\nL1367: \nL1368: When `A` is 0, the `An` part may be omitted (unless the `B` part is already omitted). When `An` is not included and `B` is non-negative, the 【340†+】 sign before `B` (when allowed) may also be omitted. In this case the syntax simplifies to just `B`.\nL1369: \nL1370: Examples:\nL1371: \nL1372:     0n+5   /* represents the 5th element in the list */\nL1373:     5      /* same */\nL1374: \nL1375: When `A` is 1 or -1, the `1` may be omitted from the rule.\nL1376: \nL1377: Examples:\nL1378: The following notations are therefore equivalent:\nL1379: \nL1380:     1n+0   /* represents all elements in the list */\nL1381:     n+0    /* same */\nL1382:     n      /* same */\nL1383: \nL1384: If `B` is 0, then every `A`th element is picked. In such a case, the `+B` (or `-B`) part may be omitted unless the `A` part is already omitted.\nL1385: \nL1386: Examples:\nL1387: \nL1388:     2n+0   /* represents every even element in the list */\nL1389:     2n     /* same */\nL1390: \nL1391: When B is negative, its minus sign replaces the 【340†+】 sign.\nL1392: \nL1393: Valid example:\nL1394: \nL1395:     3n-6\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Syntax Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/)\nciteturn2445view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/#parse-grammar\",\"lineno\":925}); Total lines: 2053\nL917:   * \"【284†Parse a list of component values】\" is for the contents of presentational attributes, which parse text into a single declaration’s value, or for parsing a stand-alone selector 【164†[SELECT]】 or list of Media Queries 【285†[MEDIAQ]】, as in 【286†Selectors API†www.w3.org】 or the `media` HTML attribute.\nL918: #### 5.4.1. Parse something according to a CSS grammar\nL919: \nL920: It is often desirable to parse a string or token list to see if it matches some CSS grammar, and if it does, to destructure it according to the grammar. This section provides a generic hook for this kind of operation. It should be invoked like \"parse `foo` as a CSS 【287†<color>】\", or similar.\nL921: This algorithm returns either failure, if the input does not match the provided grammar, or the result of parsing the input according to the grammar, which is an unspecified structure corresponding to the provided grammar specification. The return value must only be interacted with by specification prose, where the representation ambiguity is not problematic.\nL922: If it is meant to be exposed outside of spec language, the spec using the result must explicitly translate it into a well-specified representation, such as, for example, by invoking a CSS serialization algorithm (like \"serialize as a CSS 【288†<string>】 value\").\nL923: Note: This algorithm, and 【289†parse a comma-separated list according to a CSS grammar】, are usually the only parsing algorithms other specs will want to call. The remaining parsing algorithms are meant mostly for 【134†[CSSOM]】 and related \"explicitly constructing CSS structures\" cases. Consult the CSSWG for guidance first if you think you need to use one of the other algorithms.\nL924: \nL925: To parse something according to a CSS grammar (aka simply 【290†parse】) given an `input` and a CSS `grammar` production:\nL926:   1. 【291†Normalize】 `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL927:   2. 【284†Parse a list of component values】 from `input`, and let `result` be the return value.\nL928:   3. Attempt to match `result` against `grammar`. If this is successful, return the matched result; otherwise, return failure.\nL929: #### 5.4.2. Parse a comma-separated list according to a CSS grammar\nL930: \nL931: While one can definitely 【290†parse】 a value according to a grammar with commas in it, if any part of the value fails to parse, the entire thing doesn’t parse, and returns failure.\nL932: Sometimes that’s what’s desired (such as in list-valued CSS properties); other times, it’s better to let each comma-separated sub-part of the value parse separately, dealing with the parts that parse successfully one way, and the parts that fail to parse another way (typically ignoring them, such as in `【292†<img sizes>†html.spec.whatwg.org】`).\nL933: This algorithm provides an easy hook to accomplish exactly that. It returns a list of values split by \"top-level\" commas, where each values is either failure (if it failed to parse) or the result of parsing (an unspecified structure, as described in the 【290†parse】 algorithm).\nL934: \nL935: To parse a comma-separated list according to a CSS grammar (aka 【289†parse a list】) given an `input` and a CSS `grammar` production:\nL936:   1. 【291†Normalize】 `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL937:   2. If `input` contains only 【166†<whitespace-token>】s, return an empty 【259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL938:   3. 【293†Parse a comma-separated list of component values】 from `input`, and let `list` be the return value.\nL939:   4. 【294†For each†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 `item` of `list`, replace `item` with the result of 【290†parsing】 `item` with `grammar`.\nL940:   5. Return `list`.\nL941: #### 5.4.3. Parse a stylesheet\nL942: \nL943: To parse a stylesheet from an `input` given an optional 【295†url†url.spec.whatwg.org】 `location`:\nL944:   1. If `input` is a byte stream for a stylesheet, 【296†decode bytes】 from `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL945:   2. 【291†Normalize】 `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL946:   3. Create a new stylesheet, with its 【297†location】 set to `location` (or null, if `location` was not passed).\nL947:   4. 【298†Consume a stylesheet’s contents】 from `input`, and set the stylesheet’s rules to the result.\nL948: \nL949: Note: 【298†Consume a stylesheet’s contents】 always consumes the entire stream.\nL950: \nL951:   5. Return the stylesheet.\nL952: #### 5.4.4. Parse a stylesheet’s contents\nL953: \nL954: To parse a stylesheet’s contents from `input`:\nL955: \nL956:   1. 【291†Normalize】 `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL957:   2. 【298†Consume a stylesheet’s contents】 from `input`, and return the result.\nL958: \nL959: Note: 【298†Consume a stylesheet’s contents】 always consumes the entire stream.\nL960: #### 5.4.5. Parse a list of declarations\nL961: \nL962: To parse a list of declarations from `input`:\nL963: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Syntax Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/)\nciteturn2445view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/#any-value\",\"lineno\":1533}); Total lines: 2053\nL1508: All of these are exactly equivalent to 【355†<block-contents>】 in terms of parsing, but the accompanying prose only has to define validity for the categories that aren’t automatically invalid.\nL1509: \nL1510: Some examples of the various productions:\nL1511: \nL1512:   * A top-level 【118†@media】 uses 【359†<rule-list>】 for its block, while a nested one 【361†[CSS-NESTING-1]】 uses 【355†<block-contents>】.\nL1513: \nL1514:   * 【105†Style rules】 use 【355†<block-contents>】.\nL1515: \nL1516:   * 【362†@font-face】 uses 【356†<declaration-list>】.\nL1517:   * 【116†@page】 uses 【358†<declaration-rule-list>】.\nL1518: \nL1519:   * 【363†@keyframes】 uses 【357†<qualified-rule-list>】\nL1520: \nL1521: For example, the grammar for 【362†@font-face】 can be written as:\nL1522: \nL1523:     【362†<@font-face>】 = @font-face { 【356†<declaration-list>】 }\nL1524: \nL1525: and then accompanying prose defines the valid 【246†descriptors】 allowed in the block.\nL1526: The grammar for 【363†@keyframes】 can be written as:\nL1527: \nL1528:     【363†<@keyframes>】 = @keyframes { 【357†<qualified-rule-list>】 }\nL1529:     <keyframe-rule> = 【364†<keyframe-selector>】 { 【356†<declaration-list>】 }\nL1530: \nL1531: \nL1532: and then accompanying prose defines that only <keyframe-rule>s are allowed in 【363†@keyframes】, and that <keyframe-rule>s accept all animatable CSS properties, plus the 【365†animation-timing-function】 property, but they do not interact with the cascade.\nL1533: ### 7.2. Defining Arbitrary Contents: the 【366†<declaration-value>】 and 【367†<any-value>】 productions\nL1534: \nL1535: In some grammars, it is useful to accept any reasonable input in the grammar, and do more specific error-handling on the contents manually (rather than simply invalidating the construct, as grammar mismatches tend to do).\nL1536: For example, 【312†custom properties】 allow any reasonable value, as they can contain arbitrary pieces of other CSS properties, or be used for things that aren’t part of existing CSS at all. For another example, the 【368†<general-enclosed>】 production in Media Queries defines the bounds of what future syntax MQs will allow, and uses special logic to deal with \"unknown\" values.\nL1537: \nL1538: To aid in this, two additional productions are defined:\nL1539: The <declaration-value> production matches any sequence of one or more tokens, so long as the sequence does not contain 【221†<bad-string-token>】, 【218†<bad-url-token>】, unmatched 【196†<)-token>】, 【208†<]-token>】, or 【132†<}-token>】, or top-level 【130†<semicolon-token>】 tokens or 【159†<delim-token>】 tokens with a value of \"!\". It represents the entirety of what a valid declaration can have as its value.\nL1540: The <any-value> production is identical to 【366†<declaration-value>】, but also allows top-level 【130†<semicolon-token>】 tokens and 【159†<delim-token>】 tokens with a value of \"!\". It represents the entirety of what valid CSS can be in any context.\nL1541: ## 8. CSS stylesheets\nL1542: \nL1543: To parse a CSS stylesheet, first 【271†parse a stylesheet】. Interpret all of the resulting top-level 【106†qualified rules】 as 【105†style rules】, defined below.\nL1544: \nL1545: If any style rule is 【369†invalid】, or any at-rule is not recognized or is invalid according to its grammar or context, it’s a 【207†parse error】. Discard that rule.\nL1546: ### 8.1. Style rules\nL1547: \nL1548: A style rule is a 【106†qualified rule】 that associates a 【370†selector list】 with a list of property declarations and possibly a list of nested rules. They are also called 【371†rule sets†www.w3.org】 in 【372†[CSS2]】. CSS Cascading and Inheritance 【146†[CSS-CASCADE-3]】 defines how the declarations inside of style rules participate in the cascade.\nL1549: The prelude of the qualified rule is 【290†parsed】 as a 【373†<selector-list>】. If this returns failure, the entire style rule is 【369†invalid】.\nL1550: \nL1551: The content of the qualified rule’s block is parsed as a 【356†<declaration-list>】. Qualified rules in this block are also 【105†style rules】. Unless defined otherwise by another specification or a future level of this specification, at-rules in that list are 【369†invalid】 and must be ignored.\nL1552: Note: 【361†[CSS-NESTING-1]】 defines that 【374†conditional group rules】 and some other 【107†at-rules】 are allowed inside of 【105†style rules】.\nL1553: \nL1554: Declarations for an unknown CSS property or whose value does not match the syntax defined by the property are 【369†invalid】 and must be ignored. The validity of the style rule’s contents have no effect on the validity of the style rule itself. Unless otherwise specified, property names are 【219†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL1555: Note: The names of Custom Properties 【375†[CSS-VARIABLES]】 are case-sensitive.\nL1556: \nL1557: 【106†Qualified rules】 at the top-level of a CSS stylesheet are 【105†style rules】. Qualified rules in other contexts may or may not be style rules, as defined by the context.\nL1558: \nL1559: For example, qualified rules inside 【118†@media】 rules 【278†[CSS3-CONDITIONAL]】 are style rules, but qualified rules inside 【363†@keyframes】 rules 【376†[CSS3-ANIMATIONS]】 are not.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2445view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/#attr-substitution\",\"lineno\":1043}); Total lines: 2555\nL1013: If the 【562†<attr-type>】 argument is omitted, the fallback defaults to the empty string if omitted; otherwise, it defaults to the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】 if omitted.\nL1014: \nL1015: If a property contains one or more 【138†attr()】 functions, and those functions are syntactically valid, the entire property’s grammar must be assumed to be valid at parse time. It is only syntax-checked at computed-value time, after attr() functions have been 【171†substituted】.\nL1016: Note that the default value need not be of the type given. For instance, if the type required of the attribute by the author is <length>, the default could still be auto, like in 【690†width: attr(size <length>, auto);】.\nL1017: This example shows the use of attr() to visually illustrate data in an XML file:\nL1018: \nL1019:     <stock>\nL1020:       <wood length=\"12\"/>\nL1021:       <wood length=\"5\"/>\nL1022:       <metal length=\"19\"/>\nL1023:       <wood length=\"4\"/>\nL1024:     </stock>\nL1025: \nL1026:     stock::before {\nL1027:       display: block;\nL1028:       content: \"To scale, the lengths of materials in stock are:\";\nL1029:     }\nL1030:     stock > * {\nL1031:       display: block;\nL1032:       width: attr(length em, 0px);\nL1033:       height: 1em;\nL1034:       border: solid thin;\nL1035:       margin: 0.5em;\nL1036:     }\nL1037:     wood {\nL1038:       background: orange url(wood.png);\nL1039:     }\nL1040:     metal {\nL1041:       background: silver url(metal.png);\nL1042:     }\nL1043: #### 8.7.1. Substitution\nL1044: \nL1045: 【138†attr()】 is an 【126†arbitrary substitution function】, similar to 【127†var()】, and so is replaced with the value it represents (if possible) at 【173†computed value】 time; otherwise, it’s replaced with the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, which will make its declaration 【486†invalid at computed-value time】.\nL1046: \nL1047: To 【525†replace an attr() function】, given a list of `arguments`:\nL1048:   1. Let `el` be the element that the style containing the 【138†attr()】 function is being applied to. Let `first arg` be the first 【117†<declaration-value>】 in `arguments`. Let `second arg` be the <declaration-value>? passed after the comma, or null if there was no comma.\nL1049:   2. 【171†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in `first arg`, then 【160†parse】 it as 【561†<attr-name>】 【562†<attr-type>】?. If that returns failure, jump to the last step (labeled FAILURE). Otherwise, let `attr name` and `syntax` be the results of parsing (with `syntax` being null if 【562†<attr-type>】 was omitted), processed as specified in the definition of those arguments.\nL1050:   3. If `attr name` exists as an attribute on `el`, let `attr value` be its value; otherwise jump to the last step (labeled FAILURE).\nL1051: \nL1052:   4. If `syntax` is the keyword number or an 【564†<attr-unit>】 value, parse `attr value` against 【562†<attr-type>】. If that succeeds, return the result; otherwise, jump to the last step (labeled FAILURE).\nL1053: \nL1054: Note: No parsing or modification of any kind is performed on the value.\nL1055:   5. If `syntax` is null or the keyword raw-string, return a CSS 【150†<string>】 whose value is `attr value`.\nL1056: \nL1057: Note: No parsing or modification of any kind is performed on the value.\nL1058: \nL1059:   6. 【171†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in `attr value`, with «\"attribute\", `attr name`» as the 【529†substitution context】, then 【166†parse with a <syntax>】 `attr value`, with `syntax` and `el`. If that succeeds, return the result; otherwise, jump to the last step (labeled FAILURE).\nL1060: \nL1061:   7. FAILURE:\nL1062:     1. If `second arg` is null, and `syntax` was omitted, return an empty CSS 【150†<string>】.\nL1063: \nL1064:     2. If `second arg` is null, return the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL1065: \nL1066:     3. 【171†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in `second arg`, and return the result.\nL1067: #### 8.7.2. Security\nL1068: \nL1069: Tests\nL1070:   * 【691†attr-security-animation.html†wpt.fyi】 【692†(live test)†wpt.live】 【693†(source)†github.com】\nL1071:   * 【694†attr-security-if.html†wpt.fyi】 【695†(live test)†wpt.live】 【696†(source)†github.com】\nL1072:   * 【697†attr-security-transition.html†wpt.fyi】 【698†(live test)†wpt.live】 【699†(source)†github.com】\nL1073:   * 【700†attr-security.html†wpt.fyi】 【701†(live test)†wpt.live】 【702†(source)†github.com】\nL1074:   * 【703†attr-security-transition-interpolated.html†wpt.fyi】 【704†(live test)†wpt.live】 【705†(source)†github.com】\nL1075: An 【138†attr()】 function can reference attributes that were never intended by the page to be used for styling, and might contain sensitive information (for example, a security token used by scripts on the page).\nL1076: In general, this is fine. It is difficult to use 【138†attr()】 to extract information from a page and send it to a hostile party, in most circumstances. The exception to this is URLs. If a URL can be constructed with the value of an arbitrary attribute, purely from CSS, it can easily send any information stored in attributes to a hostile party, if 3rd-party CSS is allowed at all.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2445view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/#parse-with-a-syntax\",\"lineno\":241}); Total lines: 2555\nL225: The above, when parsed as a 【137†<syntax>】, would accept 【158†<percentage>】 values, 【159†<number>】 values, as well as the keyword `auto`.\nL226: \nL227:     red | <color>\nL228: \nL229: \nL230: The 【141†syntax definition†drafts.css-houdini.org】 resulting from the above 【137†<syntax>】, when used as a grammar for 【160†parsing】, would match an input `red` as an 【161†identifier】, but would match an input `blue` as a 【162†<color>】.\nL231: \nL232: The `*` 【157†<delim-token>】 represents the 【163†universal syntax definition†drafts.css-houdini.org】.\nL233: The `<transform-list>` production is a convenience form equivalent to `<transform-function>+`. Note that `<transform-list>` may not be followed by a 【146†<syntax-multiplier>】.\nL234: \nL235: 【164†Whitespace】 is not allowed between the angle bracket 【157†<delim-token>】s (`<` `>`) and the 【148†<syntax-type-name>】 they enclose, nor is whitespace allowed to precede a 【146†<syntax-multiplier>】.\nL236: \nL237: Note: The 【164†whitespace】 restrictions also apply to `<transform-list>`.\nL238: A 【144†<syntax-string>】 is a 【150†<string>】 whose value successfully 【160†parses】 as a 【137†<syntax>】, and represents the same value as that <syntax> would.\nL239: \nL240: Note: 【144†<syntax-string>】 mostly exists for historical purposes; before 【137†<syntax>】 was defined, the 【165†@property†drafts.css-houdini.org】 rule used a 【150†<string>】 for this purpose.\nL241: #### 3.3.1. Parsing as 【137†<syntax>】\nL242: \nL243: The purpose of a 【137†<syntax>】 is usually to specify how to parse another value (such as the value of a 【139†registered custom property†drafts.css-houdini.org】, or an attribute value in 【138†attr()】). However, the generic 【160†parse something according to a CSS grammar】 algorithm returns an unspecified internal structure, since parse results might be ambiguous and need further massaging.\nL244: To avoid these issues and get a well-defined result, use 【166†parse with a <syntax>】:\nL245: \nL246: To parse with a 【137†<syntax>】 given a 【167†string†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 or 【154†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 or 【168†component values】 `values`, a 【137†<syntax>】 value `syntax`, and optionally an element `el` for context, perform the following steps. It returns either CSS values, or the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL247: \nL248:   1. 【170†Parse a list of component values】 from `values`, and let `raw parse` be the result.\nL249:   2. If `el` was given, 【171†substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in `raw parse`, and set `raw parse` to that result.\nL250: \nL251:   3. 【160†parse】 `values` according to `syntax`, with a 【172†*】 value treated as `【117†<declaration-value>】?`, and let `parsed result` be the result. If `syntax` used a | combinator, let `parsed result` be the parse result from the first matching clause.\nL252: \nL253:   4. If `parsed result` is failure, return the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL254:   5. Assert: `parsed result` is now a well-defined list of one or more CSS values, since each branch of a 【137†<syntax>】 defines an unambiguous parse result (or the 【172†*】 syntax is unambiguous on its own).\nL255: \nL256:   6. Return `parsed result`.\nL257: \nL258: Note: This algorithm does not resolved the parsed values into 【173†computed values】; the context in which the value is used will usually do that already, but if not, the invoking algorithm will need to handle that on its own.\nL259: ## 4. Extensions to Level 4 Value Types\nL260: \nL261: See 【102†CSS Values and Units Level 4†www.w3.org】.\nL262: ### 4.1. Resource Locators: the 【174†<url>】 type\nL263: \nL264: See 【175†CSS Values 4 § 4.5 Resource Locators: the <url> type】.\nL265: \nL266: Tests\nL267:   * 【176†empty.html†wpt.fyi】 【177†(live test)†wpt.live】 【178†(source)†github.com】\nL268:   * 【179†fragment-only.html†wpt.fyi】 【180†(live test)†wpt.live】 【181†(source)†github.com】\nL269:   * 【182†resolve-relative-to-base.sub.html†wpt.fyi】 【183†(live test)†wpt.live】 【184†(source)†github.com】\nL270:   * 【185†resolve-relative-to-stylesheet.html†wpt.fyi】 【186†(live test)†wpt.live】 【187†(source)†github.com】\nL271:   * 【188†inline-cache-base-uri-cssom.html†wpt.fyi】 【189†(live test)†wpt.live】 【190†(source)†github.com】\nL272:   * 【191†inline-cache-base-uri.html†wpt.fyi】 【192†(live test)†wpt.live】 【193†(source)†github.com】\nL273: #### 4.1.1. Request URL Modifiers\nL274: \nL275: 【194†<request-url-modifier>】s are 【195†<url-modifier>】s that affect the 【174†<url>】’s resource 【196†request†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 by applying associated 【197†URL request modifier steps】. See 【198†CSS Values 4 § 4.5.4 URL Processing Model】.\nL276: This specification defines the following 【194†<request-url-modifier>】s:\nL277: \nL278:     【194†<request-url-modifier>】 = 【199†<cross-origin-modifier>】 【133†|】 【200†<integrity-modifier>】 【133†|】 【201†<referrer-policy-modifier>】\nL279:     【199†<cross-origin-modifier>】 = cross-origin(anonymous 【133†|】 use-credentials)\nL280:     【200†<integrity-modifier>】 = integrity(【150†<string>】)\n"
